Here's a quick self-contained test you can run with Node (or paste into a browser console):

```javascript
const zipRegex = /^[0-9]{5}(?:-[0-9]{4})?$/;

const cases = [
  // [input, expected]
  ["12345", true],
  ["12345-6789", true],
  ["00000", true],        // format only, not a real ZIP
  ["1234", false],
  ["123456", false],
  ["12345-", false],
  ["12345-67", false],
  ["123456789", false],
  ["12345-67890", false],
  ["abcde", false],
  ["12345\n", false],     // trailing newline rejected
  [" 12345", false],
];

let failed = 0;
for (const [input, expected] of cases) {
  const actual = zipRegex.test(input);
  const ok = actual === expected;
  if (!ok) failed++;
  console.log(`${ok ? "PASS" : "FAIL"}  ${JSON.stringify(input)} -> ${actual}`);
}

console.log(failed === 0 ? "\nAll tests passed" : `\n${failed} test(s) failed`);
```

**Notes:**

- In JavaScript, `\d` is already ASCII-only (`[0-9]`), so either form works. I used `[0-9]` for consistency with the earlier examples.
- Without the `m` flag, `$` in JS only matches at the very end of the string, so a trailing newline is rejected (unlike Python's `$`).
- No `g` flag on the regex, which matters because a global regex keeps `lastIndex` state between `.test()` calls and can give alternating results.

To find ZIPs inside larger text, use a global match. Lookbehind is supported in modern JS engines (Node 10+, current browsers):

```javascript
const finder = /(?<![0-9])[0-9]{5}(?:-[0-9]{4})?(?![0-9])/g;
const text = "Ship to 90210 or 10001-1234, not 1234567.";
console.log(text.match(finder)); // [ '90210', '10001-1234' ]
```

